[292.04954, "o", "\r\u001b[2KI’ll add the three complete blog article pages and a custom 404 page, then fill in the remaining shared utility styles.\r\n\r\n```\r\ncd /app/my_website && cat > blog/interpretable-neural-models.html <<'EOF'\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\r\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"How interpretability methods can help deep learning models answer scientific questions in neuroscience.\">\r\n    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#102b3f\">\r\n    <title>Building Interpretable Deep Learning Models for Neuroscience | Dr. Lydia Moreno</title>\r\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"../assets/css/styles.css\">\r\n    <script src=\"../assets/js/main.js\" defer></script>\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <a class=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main-content\">Skip to main content</a>\r\n\r\n    <header class=\"site-header\">\r\n      <div class=\"container header-inner\">\r\n        <a class=\"brand\" href=\"../index.html\" aria-label=\"Dr. Lydia Moreno — home\">\r\n          <span class=\"brand-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">LM</span>\r\n          <span class=\"brand-copy\">\r\n            <strong>Lydia Moreno</strong>\r\n            <span>Computational Neuroscience</span>\r\n          </span>\r\n        </a>\r\n        <button class=\"nav-toggle\" type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"site-nav\">\r\n          <span class=\"nav-toggle-lines\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span>\r\n          <span class=\"sr-only\">Toggle navigation</span>\r\n        </button>\r\n        <nav class=\"site-nav\" id=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Primary navigation\">\r\n          <ul>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../about.html\">About Me</a></li>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../blog.html\" aria-current=\"page\">Blog</a></li>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../publications.html\">Publications</a></li>\r\n            <li><a class=\"nav-contact\" href=\"../about.html#contact\">Contact</a></li>\r\n          </ul>\r\n        </nav>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </header>\r\n\r\n    <main id=\"main-content\">\r\n      <div class=\"container container--narrow\">\r\n        <article class=\"article-shell\">\r\n          <header class=\"article-header\">\r\n            <nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\">\r\n              <ol class=\"breadcrumbs\">\r\n                <li><a href=\"../index.html\">Home</a></li>\r\n                <li><a href=\"../blog.html\">Blog</a></li>\r\n                <li aria-current=\"page\">Interpretable models</li>\r\n              </ol>\r\n            </nav>\r\n            <p class=\"eyebrow\">Methods &amp; Interpretation</p>\r\n            <h1>Building Interpretable Deep Learning Models for Neuroscience</h1>\r\n            <div class=\"article-byline\">\r\n              <span>Lydia Moreno</span>\r\n              <span><time datetime=\"2025-03-01\">March 2025</time></span>\r\n              <span>6 min read</span>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </header>\r\n\r\n          <div class=\"article-body\">\r\n            <p>\r\n              A deep network can predict a stimulus, a behavior, or a cell type with impressive\r\n              accuracy and still leave us without a scientific explanation. In neuroscience,\r\n              prediction is rarely the final goal. We want to know which features of the data the\r\n              model relies on, whether those features correspond to known biology, and where the\r\n              model's reasoning breaks down.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Why accuracy is not enough</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Consider a model trained to decode stimulus orientation from calcium-imaging traces.\r\n              A high test-set accuracy tells us that information is present and usable. It does not\r\n              tell us whether the model learned temporal structure, exploited a recording artifact,\r\n              or simply memorized the statistics of a small set of sessions. Without targeted\r\n              analyses, each of those possibilities can produce the same headline number.\r\n            </p>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Interpretability is therefore part of experimental design, not an aesthetic addition\r\n              after training. The analysis should be chosen to test a specific scientific claim.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Attribution with a question in mind</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Feature-attribution methods can highlight input dimensions that influence a prediction.\r\n              They are most useful when the resulting attribution has a clear experimental\r\n              interpretation. For calcium traces, that may mean asking whether the model assigns\r\n              importance to a behaviorally relevant time window. For images, it may mean checking\r\n              whether model attention follows visible anatomy rather than border artifacts.\r\n            </p>\r\n            <blockquote>\r\n              An interpretation that cannot be wrong is not yet a scientific interpretation.\r\n            </blockquote>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Good practice is to pair attribution with a perturbation test: if the highlighted\r\n              feature is genuinely important, removing or altering it should change the prediction\r\n              in a predictable way. This turns a colorful heatmap into a falsifiable claim.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Representation analysis and model comparison</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Attribution explains individual predictions. Representation analysis asks a broader\r\n              question: does the model organize the data in a way that aligns with the underlying\r\n              biological variables? Linear probes, neighborhood structure, and cross-session\r\n              alignment can all provide evidence. Comparing a strong baseline with a more complex\r\n              model is especially valuable; if both reach the same accuracy but organize the data\r\n              differently, the scientific interpretation changes.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Design principles I return to</h2>\r\n            <ul>\r\n              <li>Define the scientific question before selecting an interpretability method.</li>\r\n              <li>Always include simple, transparent baselines.</li>\r\n              <li>Evaluate across sessions and animals, not only within a single recording.</li>\r\n              <li>Test interpretations with perturbations designed to falsify them.</li>\r\n              <li>Report negative evidence and unstable explanations alongside successful ones.</li>\r\n            </ul>\r\n\r\n            <p>\r\n              Interpretable models are not a separate class of algorithms. They are the result of\r\n              careful experimental choices that make a model's behavior available for scientific\r\n              scrutiny. In neuroscience, that scrutiny is where the value lies.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <p class=\"article-note\">\r\n              This post reflects my own views on research practice and is not a report of a\r\n              specific study. For related peer-reviewed work, see\r\n              <a href=\"../publications.html\">the publications page</a>.\r\n            </p>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </article>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </main>\r\n\r\n    <footer class=\"site-footer\">\r\n      <div class=\"container footer-inner\">\r\n        <p>© <span data-current-year>2025</span> Lydia Moreno · Computational Neuroscience</p>\r\n        <div class=\"footer-links\">\r\n          <a href=\"../index.html\">Home</a>\r\n          <a href=\"../blog.html\">All posts</a>\r\n          <a href=\"../publications.html\">Publications</a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </footer>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\nEOF\r\ncat > blog/ml-single-cell-genomics.html <<'EOF'\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\r\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Designing scalable and reproducible machine-learning pipelines for multimodal single-cell genomics.\">\r\n    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#102b3f\">\r\n    <title>Bridging Machine Learning and Single-Cell Genomics | Dr. Lydia Moreno</title>\r\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"../assets/css/styles.css\">\r\n    <script src=\"../assets/js/main.js\" defer></script>\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <a class=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main-content\">Skip to main content</a>\r\n\r\n    <header class=\"site-header\">\r\n      <div class=\"container header-inner\">\r\n        <a class=\"brand\" href=\"../index.html\" aria-label=\"Dr. Lydia Moreno — home\">\r\n          <span class=\"brand-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">LM</span>\r\n          <span class=\"brand-copy\">\r\n            <strong>Lydia Moreno</strong>\r\n            <span>Computational Neuroscience</span>\r\n          </span>\r\n        </a>\r\n        <button class=\"nav-toggle\" type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"site-nav\">\r\n          <span class=\"nav-toggle-lines\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span>\r\n          <span class=\"sr-only\">Toggle navigation</span>\r\n        </button>\r\n        <nav class=\"site-nav\" id=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Primary navigation\">\r\n          <ul>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../about.html\">About Me</a></li>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../blog.html\" aria-current=\"page\">Blog</a></li>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../publications.html\">Publications</a></li>\r\n            <li><a class=\"nav-contact\" href=\"../about.html#contact\">Contact</a></li>\r\n          </ul>\r\n        </nav>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </header>\r\n\r\n    <main id=\"main-content\">\r\n      <div class=\"container container--narrow\">\r\n        <article class=\"article-shell\">\r\n          <header class=\"article-header\">\r\n            <nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\">\r\n              <ol class=\"breadcrumbs\">\r\n                <li><a href=\"../index.html\">Home</a></li>\r\n                <li><a href=\"../blog.html\">Blog</a></li>\r\n                <li aria-current=\"page\">Single-cell genomics</li>\r\n              </ol>\r\n            </nav>\r\n            <p class=\"eyebrow\">Single-Cell Data</p>\r\n            <h1>Bridging Machine Learning and Single-Cell Genomics</h1>\r\n            <div class=\"article-byline\">\r\n              <span>Lydia Moreno</span>\r\n              <span><time datetime=\"2025-01-01\">January 2025</time></span>\r\n              <span>5 min read</span>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </header>\r\n\r\n          <div class=\"article-body\">\r\n            <p>\r\n              Modern single-cell experiments rarely produce one clean matrix. A study may combine\r\n              gene expression, chromatin accessibility, spatial measurements, and imaging from the\r\n              same tissue. Each modality has its own noise model, missingness pattern, and scale.\r\n              Machine learning can help integrate these views, but only when the surrounding\r\n              pipeline is designed to preserve the structure that makes the data scientifically\r\n              meaningful.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>The integration problem is also a data-engineering problem</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Before choosing a clustering algorithm or embedding method, we need a reproducible\r\n              path from raw reads to a joint representation. That path should make every filtering\r\n              decision explicit, retain sample and batch metadata, and allow an analysis to be\r\n              rerun without hidden manual steps. Workflow systems such as Nextflow and Snakemake\r\n              are valuable not because they make the statistics better, but because they make the\r\n              chain of evidence inspectable.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h3>A practical pipeline shape</h3>\r\n            <ul>\r\n              <li>Validate input files and record checksums.</li>\r\n              <li>Normalize and quality-control each modality independently.</li>\r\n              <li>Preserve donor, sample, and spatial coordinates through integration.</li>\r\n              <li>Generate a joint embedding with a documented model and random seed.</li>\r\n              <li>Evaluate clusters against held-out markers and biological annotations.</li>\r\n              <li>Export intermediate artifacts for downstream review.</li>\r\n            </ul>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Uncertainty belongs in the result</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              In single-cell analysis, a hard cell-type label can hide substantial uncertainty.\r\n              Bayesian clustering and probabilistic embeddings offer a way to carry that\r\n              uncertainty forward. A cluster that is stable across random seeds and donors is a\r\n              stronger claim than one that appears only under a particular resolution parameter.\r\n              The goal is not to eliminate subjective choices, but to expose them and test how\r\n              sensitive the conclusion is to those choices.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Reproducibility as a scientific feature</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              The most useful computational biology tools are not only accurate; they are easy to\r\n              audit. Versioned workflows, containerized environments, and clear parameters let\r\n              collaborators verify what happened. They also make it easier to reuse a pipeline\r\n              on a new tissue, species, or modality without silently carrying over assumptions.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <p>\r\n              Bridging machine learning and single-cell genomics is ultimately a matter of\r\n              connecting models to measurement. The best integrations keep the underlying\r\n              biology visible, quantify uncertainty, and make each processing decision\r\n              reproducible.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <p class=\"article-note\">\r\n              This post reflects my own views on research practice and is not a report of a\r\n              specific study. For related peer-reviewed work, see\r\n              <a href=\"../publications.html\">the publications page</a>.\r\n            </p>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </article>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </main>\r\n\r\n    <footer class=\"site-footer\">\r\n      <div class=\"container footer-inner\">\r\n        <p>© <span data-current-year>2025</span> Lydia Moreno · Computational Neuroscience</p>\r\n        <div class=\"footer-links\">\r\n          <a href=\"../index.html\">Home</a>\r\n          <a href=\"../blog.html\">All posts</a>\r\n          <a href=\"../publications.html\">Publications</a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </footer>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\nEOF\r\ncat > blog/graph-neural-networks-connectomics.html <<'EOF'\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\r\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Practical lessons for scaling graph neural networks to connectomics data and electron-microscopy reconstructions.\">\r\n    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#102b3f\">\r\n    <title>Scaling Graph Neural Networks for Connectomics | Dr. Lydia Moreno</title>\r\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"../assets/css/styles.css\">\r\n    <script src=\"../assets/js/main.js\" defer></script>\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <a class=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main-content\">Skip to main content</a>\r\n\r\n    <header class=\"site-header\">\r\n      <div class=\"container header-inner\">\r\n        <a class=\"brand\" href=\"../index.html\" aria-label=\"Dr. Lydia Moreno — home\">\r\n          <span class=\"brand-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">LM</span>\r\n          <span class=\"brand-copy\">\r\n            <strong>Lydia Moreno</strong>\r\n            <span>Computational Neuroscience</span>\r\n          </span>\r\n        </a>\r\n        <button class=\"nav-toggle\" type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"site-nav\">\r\n          <span class=\"nav-toggle-lines\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span>\r\n          <span class=\"sr-only\">Toggle navigation</span>\r\n        </button>\r\n        <nav class=\"site-nav\" id=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Primary navigation\">\r\n          <ul>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../about.html\">About Me</a></li>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../blog.html\" aria-current=\"page\">Blog</a></li>\r\n            <li><a href=\"../publications.html\">Publications</a></li>\r\n            <li><a class=\"nav-contact\" href=\"../about.html#contact\">Contact</a></li>\r\n          </ul>\r\n        </nav>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </header>\r\n\r\n    <main id=\"main-content\">\r\n      <div class=\"container container--narrow\">\r\n        <article class=\"article-shell\">\r\n          <header class=\"article-header\">\r\n            <nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\">\r\n              <ol class=\"breadcrumbs\">\r\n                <li><a href=\"../index.html\">Home</a></li>\r\n                <li><a href=\"../blog.html\">Blog</a></li>\r\n                <li aria-current=\"page\">Graph neural networks</li>\r\n              </ol>\r\n            </nav>\r\n            <p class=\"eyebrow\">Connectomics</p>\r\n            <h1>Scaling Graph Neural Networks for Connectomics</h1>\r\n            <div class=\"article-byline\">\r\n              <span>Lydia Moreno</span>\r\n              <span><time datetime=\"2024-11-01\">November 2024</time></span>\r\n              <span>5 min read</span>\r\n            </div>\r\n          </header>\r\n\r\n          <div class=\"article-body\">\r\n            <p>\r\n              Connectomics gives us the opportunity to study neural circuits as structured objects:\r\n              neurons connected by synapses, embedded in three-dimensional tissue. Graph neural\r\n              networks are a natural fit, but moving from a small proof of concept to a useful\r\n              reconstruction-scale pipeline exposes practical challenges in graph construction,\r\n              memory use, and biological validation.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Graph construction is a modeling choice</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              A neuron is not a point. It has a soma, dendrites, axons, and a complex spatial\r\n              footprint. Collapsing that geometry into a node and its synapses into edges requires\r\n              decisions about scale and representation. Should nodes represent cells or local\r\n              compartments? Should edges encode synapse count, contact area, or a learned\r\n              similarity? Those choices determine which biological patterns a model can express.\r\n            </p>\r\n            <p>\r\n              I find it useful to treat graph construction as part of the model specification and\r\n              report it explicitly, rather than hiding it inside a preprocessing script.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Sparse graphs and memory pressure</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Electron-microscopy reconstructions can contain millions of nodes and highly sparse\r\n              connectivity. Dense adjacency representations are wasteful, and neighborhood\r\n              sampling must respect the biological boundary between local motifs and long-range\r\n              projections. Mini-batch sampling, sparse message passing, and compartment-level\r\n              aggregation all help, but each introduces assumptions that should be tested against\r\n              a simpler baseline.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <blockquote>\r\n              Scalability is not only about fitting in memory; it is about preserving the\r\n              structure that makes the graph biologically interpretable.\r\n            </blockquote>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Validating graph-derived claims</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              A GNN that classifies synapse types accurately is useful. A GNN whose embeddings\r\n              reveal connectivity motifs is more scientifically interesting. To support the latter,\r\n              we need validation beyond held-out classification: stability across animals,\r\n              recovery of known circuit motifs, and sensitivity to perturbations in graph\r\n              construction. Open-source tools make these tests easier to reproduce across labs.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <h2>Why open tooling matters</h2>\r\n            <p>\r\n              Connectomics is collaborative by nature. A method becomes more valuable when other\r\n              groups can apply it to their own reconstructions, inspect failure modes, and\r\n              contribute improvements. Releasing a toolkit in the NeuroTools suite was as much\r\n              about creating a shared baseline as it was about distributing code.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <p>\r\n              Graph neural networks will not replace careful circuit analysis. Their value is in\r\n              giving us scalable, differentiable representations that can be interrogated with\r\n              biological experiments. That combination is where the most interesting work lies.\r\n            </p>\r\n\r\n            <p class=\"article-note\">\r\n              This post reflects my own views on research practice and is not a report of a\r\n              specific study. For related peer-reviewed work, see\r\n              <a href=\"../publications.html\">the publications page</a>.\r\n            </p>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </article>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </main>\r\n\r\n    <footer class=\"site-footer\">\r\n      <div class=\"container footer-inner\">\r\n        <p>© <span data-current-year>2025</span> Lydia Moreno · Computational Neuroscience</p>\r\n        <div class=\"footer-links\">\r\n          <a href=\"../index.html\">Home</a>\r\n          <a href=\"../blog.html\">All posts</a>\r\n          <a href=\"../publications.html\">Publications</a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </footer>\r\n  </body>"]
[292.049649, "o", "\r\n</html>\r\nEOF\r\ncat > 404.html <<'EOF'\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\r\n    <meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex\">\r\n    <title>Page not found | Dr. Lydia Moreno</title>\r\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"assets/css/styles.css\">\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <header class=\"site-header\">\r\n      <div class=\"container header-inner\">\r\n        <a class=\"brand\" href=\"index.html\" aria-label=\"Dr. Lydia Moreno — home\">\r\n          <span class=\"brand-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">LM</span>\r\n          <span class=\"brand-copy\"><strong>Lydia Moreno</strong><span>Computational Neuroscience</span></span>\r\n        </a>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </header>\r\n    <main id=\"main-content\" class=\"section\">\r\n      <div class=\"container container--narrow\">\r\n        <p class=\"eyebrow\">404 · Page not found</p>\r\n        <h1>That page has moved beyond the visible connectome.</h1>\r\n        <p class=\"lead\">The link may be outdated or the page may not exist.</p>\r\n        <div class=\"btn-row\">\r\n          <a class=\"btn btn--teal\" href=\"index.html\">Return home</a>\r\n          <a class=\"btn btn--ghost\" href=\"blog.html\">Browse the blog</a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </main>\r\n    <footer class=\"site-footer\">\r\n      <div class=\"container footer-inner\">\r\n        <p>© <span data-current-year>2025</span> Lydia Moreno · Computational Neuroscience</p>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </footer>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\nEOF\r\ncat >> assets/css/styles.css <<'EOF'\r\n\r\n/* Utility classes -------------------------------------------------------- */\r\n\r\n.sr-only {\r\n  position: absolute !important;\r\n  width: 1px !important;\r\n  height: 1px !important;\r\n  padding: 0 !important;\r\n  overflow: hidden !important;\r\n  clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0) !important;\r\n  white-space: nowrap !important;\r\n  border: 0 !important;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.tag-list {\r\n  display: flex;\r\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\r\n  gap: 0.45rem;\r\n  margin: 0.9rem 0 0;\r\n  padding: 0;\r\n  list-style: none;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.tag-list li {\r\n  padding: 0.35rem 0.68rem;\r\n  border: 1px solid var(--line);\r\n  border-radius: 999px;\r\n  background: var(--paper);\r\n  color: var(--ink-soft);\r\n  font-size: 0.78rem;\r\n  font-weight: 650;\r\n}\r\nEOF\r\nprintf '%s\\n' 'Created blog articles, 404 page, and utility styles.' && find . -maxdepth 3 -type f -not -path './.git/*' -print | sort\r\n```\r\n\u001b[32m⠸\u001b[0m Waiting for the LM to respond...\r\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⠸\u001b[0m Waiting for the LM to respond...\r\n\u001b[?25h\r\u001b[1A\u001b[2K"]
